[Line "5.d3 Nxc3 6.bxc3 d4: the Oxford Variation"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.d3"]
[Summary "The direct challenge to the knight. Black's accurate 6...d4 stops White's centre and equalises; the variation is worth playing mainly for the two traps in the next lines."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. d3 { [%cal Rd8h4,Rf8b4] The most direct move: the knight
must be exchanged, and White gets bxc3 and d3-d4 if he is allowed to. It also invites 5...Qh4+ and
5...Bb4, which look crushing and are not (the next two lines).

**The drawback:** the solid reply in this line equalises without risk, and 5...Bb4 can force a draw.
Goeller is blunt that 5.d3 'cannot be White's best'. Use it as a surprise weapon against opponents who
are likely to go for the check. } 5... Nxc3 6. bxc3 d4! { [%cal Rd4c3] The key move, and one many
opponents do not know. If White gets d3-d4 he has the chain c3-d4-e5 and a free attack; so Black puts
his own pawn there, fixes d3 and attacks c3. }
( 6... Be7 7. Nf3 O-O 8. d4 { [%cal Gf1d3,Ge1g1] What White is hoping for: Bd3, O-O and a kingside
attack in the style of the French Advance, with the f-file thrown in. } )
7. Nf3 Nc6
( 7... c5 8. Be2 dxc3 9. O-O Nc6 10. Qe1 { Not 10.Ng5 Nxe5, as in Seret-Spassky, Angers 1990. }
10... Nd4 11. Bd1 Be7 12. Qxc3 O-O { Equal. } )
8. Be2 { White ignores c3 and gets castled; the pawn on d4 is the one he wants to remove. }
( 8. cxd4 { The old main line simply liquidates, and the game is level and simplified. } 8... Bb4+
9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 Nxd4 )
8... dxc3 { In practice 8...Bc5, keeping the pawn on d4, is played as often. } 9. d4 { The centre is
built after all, at the cost of a pawn. } 9... Nb4 10. a3 Nd5 11. O-O Be7 { Bronstein-Malaniuk, Hastings
1996.

**Verdict:** equal. White has the centre and the f-file for the pawn on c3, which is going nowhere;
Black has a superb knight on d5 and no weaknesses. } *

[Line "5.d3 Bb4: the king walk to e3"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.d3"]
[Summary "Black sacrifices the knight for a check on h4. White's king marches to e3, and with exact play the game ends in a repetition."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. d3 Bb4 { [%cal Rd8h4] Leaves the knight to be taken on
purpose: the pin on c3 and the check on h4 are the compensation. } 6. dxe4 Qh4+ 7. Ke2 { The only
move. }
( 7. g3?? { Blocks the check and loses a rook. } 7... Qxe4+ { The c3 knight is pinned and cannot take
the queen, so h1 falls. } )
7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 Bg4+ 9. Nf3 dxe4 10. Qd4 { [%cal Gd4e4] The queen comes out with tempo against e4 and
supports the king's walk. } 10... Bh5 { Keeps the pin and makes room for ...Qg4. } 11. Ke3 { The king
steps out of the pin and attacks e4 itself. On 11.Kd2 Black has 11...Qg4. } 11... Bxf3 12. gxf3
( 12. Bb5+ { The usual attempt to avoid the draw. After 12...c6 13.gxf3 the play is very sharp and
theory has never given a clear verdict; only for those who have prepared it. } )
12... Qe1+ 13. Kf4 Qh4+ 14. Ke3 { Drawn by repetition, Nunn-Speelman, London 1980. Makarychev-Razuvaev,
USSR Championship 1979, ended the same way after 11...exf3 12.gxf3 Qe1+ 13.Kf4.

**Verdict:** a draw whenever Black wants one, which Goeller counts as good evidence that 5.d3 is not
White's most ambitious move. } *

[Line "5.d3 Qh4+: the Würzburger Trap"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.d3"]
[Summary "The check that 5.d3 invites. White gives up the h1 rook, wins it back on a8 and comes out on top; the famous finish with a trapped bishop on c2 is one version."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. d3 Qh4+? { The move every opponent sees first, and the
only real reason to play 5.d3. } 6. g3 Nxg3 7. Nf3 { Developing with tempo on the queen. The knight
cannot be taken (7.hxg3 Qxh1), so White lets it take the rook and counts on what the c3 knight does
next. } 7... Qh5 8. Nxd5!
{ [%cal Gd5c7,Gd5f4] The point. Nxc7+ and Nf4 are both threatened, and Black cannot meet both and save
the knight on g3. } 8... Bg4 { The best practical try: a pin against the pin. }
( 8... Nxh1? { Taking the rook. } 9. Nxc7+ Kd8 10. Nxa8 Be7 11. Bg2 { White collects the h1 knight as
well and is winning (Dobosz-Ganev 1971). } )
( 8... Na6 { Covers c7. } 9. Nf4 Qh6 10. Ne2 { [%cal Gc1h6] A discovered attack on the queen, and the
g3 knight is lost. } )
9. Bg2 { The recommendation of the Encyclopaedia, giving White the better game. The rook is defended,
the pin on f3 is broken and both Nf4 and Nxc7+ are still in the air. }
( 9. Nf4 { The move that gave the trap its name. If Black takes on c2 at the end, 12.b3 shuts the
bishop in and Kd2 or Rh2 collects it. It works only if Black is greedy, which is why 9.Bg2 is
preferred. } 9... Bxf3 10. Nxh5 Bxd1 11. hxg3 Bxc2 12. b3 ) *
